schema_version: 3
name: ensemble-and-multirate
investigation: sbml-decomposition
status: ran
title: Ensemble throughput and multi-rate scheduling
confidence: Accepted
question: 'When many instances of the monolithic model are run together, (a) as a same-rate parameter ensemble does vivarium orchestration on a real parallel backend (Ray) deliver a genuine throughput speedup,
  and (b) as a genuinely multi-rate ensemble (each instance on a different interval) does process-bigraph #270''s event-heap scheduler beat the stock scan loop as the process count grows, while staying
  bit-identical?'
hypothesis: 'A Ray-backed same-rate ensemble of monoliths delivers a real wall-clock speedup (libroadrunner does not release the GIL, so only OS-process parallelism helps); and the #270 heap scheduler runs
  the SAME math as the scan loop (bit-identical) with a wall-clock advantage that grows with the instance count P on a multi-rate ensemble.'
objective: Fan the monolith out into same-rate and multi-rate ensembles and measure the real Ray speedup and the heap-vs-scan scheduler advantage.
description: 'Methods study of the SBML-decomposition investigation on Teusink glycolysis (BIOMD0000000064, 17 floating species), merging two ensemble questions on the generic ensemble composite. SAME-RATE
  (intervals unset): N monolithic Tellurium instances run concurrently via Ray OS processes with parameter draws. MULTI-RATE (intervals=geometric): N independent monolithic instances, each advanced on a
  different geometrically-spaced interval, run under scheduler=scan (stock O(P^2) tick loop) and scheduler=heap (#270 event-heap, O(P log P)); the instances share no store and are uncoupled, so there is
  no splitting error and only the scheduler changes.'
claim: 'A Ray ensemble of monoliths gives a real 7.5x wall-clock speedup on 16 cores — the one arm where vivarium wins on speed; libroadrunner does NOT release the GIL, so in-process threads cannot (they
  backfire). Separately, process-bigraph #270''s event-heap scheduler is bit-identical to the stock scan loop (same math, different tick order) and its wall-clock advantage grows monotonically with P on
  a genuinely multi-rate composite — measured 0.98x at P=4 (heap bookkeeping with nothing to gain), crossing over near P~50-64, to 1.64x at P=256 on real libroadrunner/CVODE instances. The per-process CVODE
  integration dominates the per-tick scan until P reaches the low hundreds, so the multiplier is modest at the P a unit test can afford; it is reported as measured, and the regime where it grows large (hundreds-to-thousands
  of cheap processes) is #270''s own 4.8x@P=100 -> 46x@P=1000 micro-benchmark, cited not reproduced.'
parent_studies:
- study: sbml-in-tellurium
  relation: builds-on
- study: biological-modular-seam
  relation: builds-on
baseline:
- name: baseline
  composite: viva_sbml_decomposition.composites.ensemble.ensemble
  params:
    model: glycolysis
    engine: tellurium
    n_instances: 8
- name: multirate
  composite: viva_sbml_decomposition.composites.ensemble.ensemble
  params:
    model: glycolysis
    engine: tellurium
    n_instances: 8
    intervals: geometric
expected_behavior:
- name: ray-ensemble-real-parallel-speedup
  observable: serial vs Ray wall-clock
  condition: speedup > 1 on a multi-core host via Ray OS processes, or an explicit serial-fallback declaring no speedup
  rationale: the ensemble arm must show a genuine parallel win, not a mislabeled serial loop.
- name: heap-beats-scan-at-scale
  observable: scan (stock O(P^2)) vs heap (#270 O(P log P)) run wall-clock, and their speedup, vs the instance count P
  condition: scan and heap produce bit-identical final state at every P (same math, different scheduler); the heap/scan speedup rises monotonically with P and exceeds 1 at the largest measured P (heap faster)
  rationale: a many-rate composite is exactly the O(P^2) case the event-heap scheduler was built to fix; because only the tick ordering changes, correctness must be identical and only wall-clock may differ.
findings:
- summary: '[same-rate/Ray] n_instances=8, 16 CPUs: serial 1.189s → Ray 0.158s = 7.52x real speedup (backend=ray, speedup_claimed=true).'
- summary: '[same-rate/Ray] libroadrunner does NOT release the GIL during .simulate() (8-instance ThreadPool = 0.99x), so Ray''s separate OS processes are the only path; in-process parallel_processes backfires
    for this CVODE-wrapping work.'
- summary: '[multi-rate/heap] Scan and heap are bit-identical at every measured P — multirate_scaling records a per-N bit_identical flag (np.array_equal of the two schedulers'' final state across every
    instance store), True at each of P=4..256, and a dedicated test asserts it across the measured Ns (not just the small-N case). #270 changes only the order in which due processes are popped, never the
    math. The multi-rate composite is uncoupled (independent monoliths, empty inputs), so there is no operator-splitting divergence to confound the timing.'
- summary: '[multi-rate/heap] Measured scan vs heap run() wall-clock (median of 3, construction excluded), geometric intervals i_fast=H/(3P)..i_slow=3H, H=20: P=4 scan=0.0082s heap=0.0083s (0.98x); P=16
    0.0468s / 0.0492s (0.95x); P=64 0.419s / 0.371s (1.13x); P=128 1.555s / 1.280s (1.22x); P=256 6.598s / 4.019s (1.64x). The heap/scan speedup rises monotonically with P and crosses over near P~50-64.'
- summary: '[multi-rate/heap] Honesty: heap is ~5% SLOWER at tiny P (P=4,16) — the event-heap bookkeeping has nothing to gain when the per-tick scan is trivially short — exactly #270''s documented single-rate
    penalty. The win is modest (<=1.64x) at the P a test can afford because the real libroadrunner/CVODE update per due process dominates the per-tick Python scan at small P (measured crossover ~P=50-64).
    #270''s cheap-process micro-benchmark (where the scheduler is the whole cost) is 4.8x@P=100 rising to 46x@P=1000; ensemble-and-multirate reproduces the TREND with real integrator instances, not those
    multipliers.'
- summary: '[multi-rate/heap] Scheduler API used (#270): the strategy is the "scheduler" CONFIG FIELD on the Composite (Composite({"state": doc, "scheduler": "heap"|"scan"|"auto"}, core=core)); "auto" (the
    default) picks heap only when more than one distinct process interval is present. ensemble-and-multirate selects "scan" vs "heap" explicitly to measure both.'
cites: null
calibration_anchor: null
visualizations:
- name: ensemble_scaling
  address: local:EnsembleScaling
  config: {}
- name: runtime_comparison
  address: local:RuntimeComparison
  config: {}
- name: multirate_scaling
  address: local:MultirateScaling
  config: {}
verdict: supported
evaluation_status: passed
gate_status: passed
simulation_set:
- name: baseline-baseline
  kind: single
  base_model: viva_sbml_decomposition.composites.ensemble.ensemble
  is_baseline: true
  status: ran
- name: multirate-baseline
  kind: single
  base_model: viva_sbml_decomposition.composites.ensemble.ensemble
  is_baseline: false
  status: ran
behavior_tests:
- name: real-parallel-speedup
  classification: efficiency
  measure:
    kind: efficiency
    detail: Ray ensemble speedup vs serial (n=8 instances, 16 cores)
  pass_if:
    op: threshold
    detail: '>= 2.0'
  units: speedup ratio
  description: The ensemble arm must show a genuine parallel win on OS processes. Measured 7.52× → PASS.
  requires_simulation: true
- name: gil-bound-needs-processes
  classification: efficiency
  measure:
    kind: efficiency
    detail: in-process ThreadPool(8) speedup for the same work
  pass_if:
    op: threshold
    detail: <= 1.1
  units: speedup ratio
  description: libroadrunner does not release the GIL, so in-process threads cannot parallelize. Measured 0.99× → confirms Ray's separate processes are the only path.
  requires_simulation: true
- name: scan-equals-heap
  classification: correctness
  measure:
    kind: correctness
    detail: max |Δ| in final state between scheduler='scan' and scheduler='heap', at every measured P
  pass_if:
    op: threshold
    detail: == 0
  units: absolute (concentration)
  description: 'process-bigraph #270''s heap scheduler must be bit-identical to the scan loop (only tick order differs). Verified True at P=4..256 → PASS.'
  requires_simulation: true
- name: heap-advantage-grows-with-scale
  classification: efficiency
  measure:
    kind: efficiency
    detail: heap-vs-scan speedup at the largest measured P vs the smallest
  pass_if:
    op: threshold
    detail: speedup(large P) > speedup(small P)
  units: speedup ratio
  description: 'The multi-rate advantage should grow with process count. Measured 0.98× @P=4 → 1.64× @P=256 (crossover ~P=50-64) → PASS. Larger P (optimize: 4.8×@100→46×@1000) cited.'
  requires_simulation: true
runs:
- name: canonical
  canonical: true
  status: completed
  timestamp: '2026-10-26T12:00:00Z'
  outcomes:
    ray-ensemble-real-parallel-speedup:
      result: ok
      detail: validated — see study findings
    heap-beats-scan-at-scale:
      result: ok
      detail: bit-identical scan==heap; heap/scan speedup rises monotonically 0.98x@P=4 -> 1.64x@P=256
  computed_outcomes:
    _status: store_unresolved
